# HTF Regime Navigator [HRN]

- url: https://www.tradingview.com/script/fhmb3zSU-HTF-Regime-Navigator-HRN/
- type: indicator
- pine_id: PUB;441bc6fb13a246e8851ca099df95b177
- version: 7.0

## Description

HTF Regime Navigator [HRN]

█ OVERVIEW

The HTF Regime Navigator is a top-down market analysis dashboard built on one premise: markets spend most of their time in ranges, and a trend has to be earned. It is not a buy/sell arrow generator. It answers three questions, in order, and shows its reasoning for each:

1. What environment are we in? (the Regime engine)
2. Which way does the weight of evidence lean? (a fully transparent, user-weighted scoring model expressed as Bull / Base / Bear percentages)
3. Where is the high-quality price to act? (EMA–Fibonacci confluence — "a place to do business")

"HTF" means higher timeframe: the tool is designed for traders who set their bias on the weekly or daily chart and execute lower, though every module is timeframe-agnostic and works on any liquid symbol.

█ WHO IT'S FOR

• Swing and position traders who form their bias on the weekly or daily chart and execute lower — the defaults, the workflow, and the dashboard's top-down read order are all built around that sequence.

• Range and reversal traders: anyone who buys pullbacks into value (the Fibonacci golden zone, EMA retests, Wyckoff springs) rather than chasing breakouts. Most of the machinery exists to identify what kind of range you are in and where the high-quality prices sit inside it.

• Traders who think in probabilities and scenarios rather than binary calls. The forecast is expressed as three weighted cases — Bull, Base, Bear — and the Action row says STAND ASIDE far more often than it suggests positioning.

• Process-oriented traders who want to see the reasoning. Every classification and every probability decomposes into visible, re-weightable components, so the tool suits people who want to audit and tune a model, not follow arrows.

• Discretionary traders who do their own research: the Discretionary Overlay feeds macro, catalyst, flow, and consensus work into the same probability engine as the chart evidence.

Who it is not for: scalpers wanting high-frequency intraday signals, or anyone looking for a black-box buy/sell system. The fractal-based structure confirms with a deliberate lag, the engine is designed to keep you out of the market most of the time, and it will not do your thinking for you.

█ THE LOGIC BEHIND THE INDICATOR

Every module encodes one of five working assumptions about how markets behave:

1. Consolidation is the default state. Markets spend the large majority of their time ranging; genuine trends are the exception. The burden of proof is therefore inverted: instead of asking "is this a trend?", the engine assumes range until a breakout is confirmed by the moving-average stack in both side and slope. This single prior removes the most expensive class of error — treating every breakout as the start of a trend.

2. Regime is permission, forecast is bias, confluence is location and trigger. Three separate questions, answered in order. The regime decides which trades are even on the table. The forecast decides which direction deserves the benefit of the doubt, and how strongly. Confluence decides where acting on that bias offers favorable prices. The layers are kept independent on purpose, so a disagreement between them (for example, a bullish confluence lighting up against a bearish lean) is information rather than noise.

3. Evidence moves probabilities; it never creates certainty. The forecast starts from a base-case prior — "nothing resolves, the chop continues" — and each piece of evidence (structure, regime, entry value, divergence, Wyckoff behavior, your own research) shifts probability away from that prior in proportion to the weight you assign it. A hard floor keeps the base case alive no matter how one-sided the evidence looks, because chop is always possible. This is why the output is three scenario probabilities rather than a signal.

4. Location matters as much as direction. A correct bias executed at a bad price is still a bad trade. The Entry Value component scores extended price negatively even when the bias is bullish, and the confluence engine only lights up where independent forms of support overlap — moving averages sitting inside the Fibonacci value zone, ideally with price there too and slopes turning. That overlap is the "place to do business."

5. The best reversal entries occur where structure has just been recovered. A Wyckoff spring is a failed breakdown — a shakeout that closes back inside the range, showing supply exhausted below. A fresh EMA reclaim is the same event expressed in moving-average terms. The optional signal engine is built entirely around this: it requires freshly recovered structure (a recent slope flip, a fresh reclaim, or a qualified spring) and then triggers on the reclaim itself or the first tests of the fast EMA afterward — never on stale, mid-trend states.

Because each assumption is implemented as visible, weighted, adjustable components, the tool doubles as a measurable model: when it is wrong, the Breakdown table shows which component was wrong, and you can re-weight accordingly.

█ WHY THIS COMBINATION IS ORIGINAL AND USEFUL

HRN uses classic building blocks — multi-timeframe EMAs, Williams fractals, Wyckoff springs/upthrusts, RSI divergence, Fibonacci retracements, and a volume profile. What makes it original is that these are not stacked side by side; they are wired into a single decision engine with an explicit consolidation prior:

• One structure feeds everything. The Williams-fractal range defines the structure every other module measures against: springs and upthrusts are validated against the range's edges and height, the Fibonacci golden zone anchors to it by default, and the Entry Value score is price's position within it.

• Trends must be earned. The regime engine refuses to call a trend on a breakout alone — the breakout must be confirmed by a user-set number of EMAs agreeing in both side and slope. Until then, the market is classified as a range, and the engine's job becomes determining what kind of range it is (accumulation, distribution, or neutral) by counting independent evidence.

• Evidence becomes probabilities through an honest prior. Six components (MA Structure, Regime, Entry Value, HTF Divergence, Wyckoff, Discretionary Overlay) are each normalized to −100…+100, weighted by inputs you control, and mapped to Bull / Base / Bear percentages through a base-case prior with a hard floor. The model is structurally prevented from claiming near-certainty, because chop is always possible.

• The layers gate each other. The confluence highlight and the optional mechanical signal engine both consume the same canonical golden zone; the signal engine is vetoed by the regime (no long signals in a confirmed downtrend by default) and assisted by Wyckoff events (a recent qualified spring satisfies the reversal-freshness requirement for longs, an upthrust for shorts).

• Everything is auditable. The Forecast Breakdown table shows score × weight = contribution for every component, so you can see exactly why the model leans the way it does — and re-weight it when you disagree. The tool is meant to be tuned like a model, not trusted like a black box.

That integration — one structural range feeding one weighted evidence model feeding one location filter, under an explicit "consolidation is the default" prior — is not reproducible by simply adding the individual indicators to a chart.

█ HOW IT WORKS

1 — HTF EMA Stack. Six independent EMA slots, each with its own timeframe, length, color, and toggle (defaults: chart 50/100/200 plus a weekly 50 visible; a weekly 200 and daily 50 are configured but off). For each active EMA the tool tracks which side price is on and the EMA's own slope, measured against its value a configurable number of higher-timeframe bars back.

2 — HTF Range (Williams fractals). Pivot highs/lows with configurable wings on a selectable range timeframe (weekly by default) define the range high and low. A breakout requires the range-timeframe close to exceed the boundary by a buffer percentage. A context lookback checks where price came from: falling into the range is an accumulation clue; rising into it, a distribution clue.

3 — Wyckoff engine. A spring is only accepted when the undercut penetrates no more than a maximum percentage of the range height, the bar closes back inside the range, the range is not already broken down, optional volume qualification passes (a quiet low-volume test, or stopping volume above a multiple of average), and a cooldown since the last signal has elapsed. Upthrusts mirror this at the range high. Separately, the up- versus down-bar volume share over a lookback gives the range an accumulation or distribution character (effort vs. result).

4 — HTF Divergence. Regular RSI divergence detected at confirmed pivots on a selectable timeframe (weekly by default).

5 — Regime engine. If price has broken out AND at least N active EMAs confirm the direction (correct side + correct slope), the regime is TRENDING UP or TRENDING DOWN. Otherwise the engine counts the bullish vs. bearish evidence above (range context, volume share, spring/upthrust, divergence) and classifies ACCUMULATION, DISTRIBUTION, or NEUTRAL RANGE, with a confidence read based on how far apart the evidence counts are.

6 — Forecast. Six components, each scored −100…+100:
• MA Structure — price's position across the active EMA stack.
• Regime — the classification's directional lean.
• Entry Value — how favorable current price is for the bias. It deliberately scores extended/expensive price negatively, even in a bullish setup — it exists to fight chasing.
• HTF Divergence — recent bullish/bearish divergence.
• Wyckoff — recent spring/upthrust plus the volume-share character.
• Discretionary Overlay — four dropdowns you set from your own research (macro liquidity, upcoming catalysts, insider/institutional flow, analyst consensus), since Pine cannot read the news. Neutral by default.
The weighted sum produces a Net Score (−100…+100), which is mapped to Bull / Base / Bear percentages through a base-case prior (default 55%), a sensitivity, and a floor (default 15%). An Action row only suggests positioning when a case clears the actionable threshold (default 65%); otherwise it reads STAND ASIDE.

7 — Fibonacci engine. Full retracement and extension sets, each level individually toggleable, editable, and colorable. The anchor is selectable: the HTF Range (default), the last detected Swing High/Low, or a Lookback High/Low. Orientation follows the forecast bias automatically or can be forced Uptrend/Downtrend. The canonical 38.2–61.8 golden zone drives entry detection regardless of which display levels you show, and the zone can be filled in one or two bands.

8 — EMA-Fib Confluence. When the selected EMAs sit inside or near the golden zone (tolerance measured as a percentage of the fib range), optionally requiring price in the zone and an EMA slope filter, the chart paints a background band and the dashboard glows green or red. This is the location cue — the "pay attention" state.

9 — Signal engine (optional). A mechanical fresh-reversal detector: per-EMA tri-state classification (above / testing / below, with a % or ATR-fraction tolerance), reclaim tracking, slope-alignment freshness windows, a first-K-tests-after-reclaim route (the first pullback test can come weeks after a reclaim, so it is counted in episodes, not bars), optional EMA stack-order requirement, Wyckoff assist, higher-timeframe regime veto, optional volume gate, confirmation closes, cooldowns, and invalidation on a close beyond the lowest (or highest) signal EMA plus a buffer. A 0–100 confluence score is always computed and can optionally gate entries. State machine: SETUP → PENDING → CONFIRMED → HOLDING → INVALIDATED, with fully customizable chart markers.

10 — Volume Profile. Up to 100 rows over up to 1500 bars, POC / VAH / VAL lines and labels, optional up/down volume split per row.

11 — Dashboard + Forecast Breakdown tables. Every row can be toggled off to shrink either table. The whole-table tint source is selectable: EMA-Fib Confluence (default), Signal Engine, Both, or Off.

12 — Alerts. One dynamic alert() covers every toggled event with a rich message (regime change, spring/upthrust, HTF divergence, entry zone + bias alignment, probability threshold crosses, confluence start, signal confirmed, setup forming). Individual alertcondition() hooks are also provided. Create a single alert on the indicator with "Any alert() function call" to receive all enabled events.

13 — Data bus. A few hidden (display.none) plots expose the regime code and key event streams so that other indicators on the same chart can read them as an external source input.

█ HOW TO USE IT

The 30-second read, top to bottom:
1. Is the dashboard glowing green/red, or is there a background band on the chart? That means a confluence area is live right now.
2. Regime row — trend or range, which kind, and at what confidence. A Low confidence means the evidence is nearly tied; treat it as a hint, not a conclusion.
3. Range position — is price cheap (low in the range) or expensive (high in the range)?
4. EMA-Fib Conf row — if confluence isn't firing, the detail cell tells you exactly which condition is missing (E1/E2/E3 = EMAs in zone, P = price in zone, S = slope filter).
5. Bull / Base / Bear percentages and the Action row — the bottom line.

A suggested top-down workflow: set the bias on the weekly (regime + the weekly EMA slope), drop to the daily to find the setup, and only act where the confluence band lights inside the golden zone. Honor the Entry Value component — it penalizes chasing on purpose. Fill in the Discretionary Overlay from your own research; it is neutral until you do, and it flows into the Net Score at the weight you assign it.

Note on the Fibonacci Auto direction: because the orientation follows the forecast bias, it can flip while regime confidence is low. If you have already made the directional call, force the Direction to Uptrend or Downtrend to lock the levels in place.

█ REPAINTING AND CONFIRMATION

• Every request.security() call uses lookahead_off.
• Fractals and divergence pivots confirm a fixed number of bars after the extreme (the Williams definition). They are late by design and are never repainted retroactively.
• Higher-timeframe values update while the live HTF bar forms and finalize at its close — treat reads as firm at the close of the relevant bar.
• Dynamic alerts fire on confirmed bars only.

█ SETTINGS NOTES

Every input carries a tooltip explaining what it does and what its default means. The highest-leverage settings are: MAs Required to Confirm Trend (the consolidation-first switch), the six Forecast Weights, the Probability Mapping group (prior, sensitivity, floor, actionable threshold), the Fibonacci Anchor Source and Direction, the EMA-Fib Confluence conditions, and — if you enable the signal row — the Entry Trigger mode, tolerance mode (% of price vs. ATR fraction), confirmation closes, and cooldown.

█ LIMITATIONS

• This is an indicator (decision support), not a strategy: it produces no performance statistics, and its Bull / Base / Bear percentages are the output of a transparent, user-weighted scoring model — heuristic evidence weighting you control, not a statistical prediction of returns.
• The Discretionary Overlay only contributes what you put into it.
• Fractal-based structure confirms with a deliberate lag; the tool trades immediacy for reliability.
• Defaults assume daily/weekly charts on liquid symbols; intraday works, but the higher-timeframe modules are less in their element there.
• Drawing objects (lines, labels, boxes) are pooled with configurable caps; the oldest objects are removed first.

█ DISCLAIMER

This script and its description are for educational and informational purposes only and do not constitute financial advice. No indicator predicts market outcomes. The tool is built to make its reasoning fully inspectable so that you can evaluate, adjust, and own your decisions. Always do your own research and manage your own risk.

## Source Code

````pine
//@version=6
indicator("HTF Regime Navigator", shorttitle="HRN v1.8.9", overlay=true, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500, max_boxes_count=500, max_bars_back=1500)

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.9 — MARKER OFFSET FROM PRICE
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Signal and Wyckoff markers anchored EXACTLY at each bar's high/low, so on
// dense charts they sat inside the candle range — busy to look at and hard to
// hover for the v1.8.7/v1.8.8 tooltips. Two new ATR-denominated offsets push
// them clear: "Marker Offset (ATR)" in Signal Display for the six signal
// markers, "Marker Offset (ATR)" in Wyckoff Markers for SPRING/UT labels.
// ATR-denominated so one setting scales across symbols and timeframes; 0.0
// (default) is byte-identical to v1.8.8. Below-bar placements are floored at
// a positive price so a large offset on a cheap symbol cannot degenerate the
// label on a log-scale chart. No signal logic changed.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.8 — TRADE RESULT ON THE EXIT MARKER
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Hovering a SIGNAL END marker now shows the completed trade: entry price and
// date, exit price and date, the delta in % and in R (measured against the
// stop that was live AT ENTRY), MFE/MAE excursions, bars held, route, and the
// exit path. The Signal Ended alert carries a one-line version of the same.
//
// DEFINITION OF "RESULT" — read once, it matters:
//  • Entry = the CLOSE of the confirmation bar (the signal's own timestamp).
//    Exit = the CLOSE of the ending bar. This is the SIGNAL auditing itself,
//    close-to-close; your fills will differ.
//  • R uses the stop AT ENTRY, frozen. A structural stop that later walked up
//    with the EMAs does not retroactively shrink the risk you took at entry.
//  • An invalidation that gaps through the stop reports the REAL exit close,
//    so a loss can exceed −1R. That is gap-aware truth, not an error — the
//    same convention as the Expectancy Lab's brackets.
//  • MFE/MAE use the highs/lows from the entry bar through the exit bar.
// No signal logic changed. Requires "Chart Markers on SIGNAL END" to be ON
// to see the hover; the alert enhancement works regardless.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.7 — SELF-AUDITING ENTRY & SETUP MARKERS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Hovering a CONFIRMED entry marker now shows the trade data the engine knew
// at the moment it fired: route, confluence score, all six gate flags, the
// structural stop with its distance and reference, the regime, and the
// forecast probabilities. SETUP markers get a lighter version (score, arming
// license, gate flags). Exit markers already had this from v1.8.3; the entry
// markers — where the data matters most — did not.
//
// The tooltip is a FROZEN snapshot taken at confirmation. The stop reference
// itself keeps moving with the EMAs and fibs afterwards, so an old marker's
// tooltip can legitimately disagree with the live dashboard row on the same
// trade — that is the audit property, not a bug, and the tooltip says so.
// This gives HRN the same self-auditing markers as RSI Divergence Pro.
// Toggleable (default ON); no signal logic changed.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.6 — FIB STOP LEVELS (23.6 / CUSTOM / AUTO)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The fib pair SWAPS ROLES when the signal orientation flips, so a fixed level
// is the correct stop only half the time. With a 71.05-93.57 range:
//     orientation UP    23.6% = 88.26   78.6% = 75.87   -> 78.6 is the deep one
//     orientation DOWN   23.6% = 76.36   78.6% = 88.75   -> 23.6 is the deep one
// Under "Structure Only" the orientation follows the regime, so a shift into
// DISTRIBUTION inverts which level sits under price — and a stop pinned to
// 78.6% then lands ABOVE price, which is not a stop at all.
//
//  • Stop Reference gains "Fib 23.6%", "Fib Custom %" (any level, own float
//    input), and "Fib Auto (protective)" — the last always takes whichever of
//    the 23.6/78.6 pair sits on the protective side of the trade, so it stays
//    correct through every orientation flip without being re-set by hand.
//  • Invalidation Reference gains "Match Stop Reference", so the state machine
//    can invalidate at the level you actually treat as the stop rather than at
//    the lowest signal EMA. The EMA references carry the Invalidation Buffer;
//    the fib levels do not — build room in with Fib Custom % (e.g. 82 instead
//    of 78.6) rather than double-buffering a level that is already exact.
//  • All fib stops are guarded to positive prices, so a custom level beyond
//    100% cannot project a zero or negative stop onto the bus.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.5 — STOP LEVEL + INLINE SLOPES ON THE DASHBOARD
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  • STOP LEVEL. v1.8 added a selectable structural stop and published it on
//    the bus, but there was nowhere on screen to read it — you had to wire the
//    Expectancy Lab to see your own stop. While a signal is CONFIRMED or
//    HOLDING the Signal row now ends with the live stop and its distance from
//    price, e.g. "⊣ 231.40 (4.2%)". The entry score it replaces describes
//    entry quality, which is already locked in once you are in the position;
//    the stop is the number that matters every bar afterwards. Selectable:
//    Score / Stop When In Position (default) / Both.
//  • INLINE SLOPES (default OFF). Appends the measured slope percentages of
//    the slope-member EMAs directly after the S flag — "S✗ 0.42/0.18%" — so
//    the number that decides the gate sits next to the verdict instead of
//    behind a hover. Off by default because it is a calibration aid: you set
//    Min Slope % once, then you want the row narrow again.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.4 — CONFLUENCE DIRECTION SOURCE
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The EMA-Fib confluence could read BEAR (red band, red table tint) with price
// above every EMA and every slope positive. It was never measuring the EMAs:
//     confBull = slopeDir=="Positive" ? true
//              : slopeDir=="Negative" ? false
//              : fibUp                              <- "Either" lands here
//     fibUp    = biasStickyBull                     <- Auto fib direction
//     biasStickyBull = regimeDir > 0 ? true : regimeDir < 0 ? false : ...
// so with Slope Direction on "Either" the colour was inherited from the fib
// orientation, which is inherited from the REGIME. A DISTRIBUTION regime
// therefore painted the confluence bearish no matter what the EMAs were doing.
//
// NEW "Confluence Direction Source" (default EMA Slopes) derives the direction
// from something the confluence actually measures: the slopes of the EMAs in
// the band, price versus the EMA stack, both together, the regime, or the old
// fib-orientation behavior. Note this is DIRECTION only — the Slope Direction
// dropdown remains the FILTER, and on "Either" that filter still accepts any
// non-zero slope by design.
//
// Also added: optional whole-table tint while a signal is HOLDING, not only
// while it is freshly CONFIRMED — a HOLDING long against a red confluence tint
// was previously unreadable.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.3 — EXIT MARKER STYLING
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The SIGNAL END marker introduced in v1.8 inherited the SIDE colour for both
// its background AND its text — a lime glyph on a lime panel for longs, red on
// red for shorts — so it vanished into the entry marker it was meant to
// contrast with. It now has its own full colour set, defaulting to a WHITE
// glyph on a neutral dark panel so it reads against green, red and background
// tints alike. Six new controls: background and text colour per side, plus
// shared alpha and size. The marker also carries a hover tooltip naming the
// exit path (invalidated vs structure lost) and the entry route it closed.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.2 — STRICT MODE + SLOPE THRESHOLD
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Two separate causes of "it fired but the EMAs are clearly falling".
//
// 1) THE LICENSE ARCHITECTURE WAS NEVER A CONJUNCTION. Since v1.3 a "fresh
//    license" (reclaim / fresh stack / spring) has BYPASSED the slope gate and
//    the zone gate outright:
//        slopeGateUp = slopeGenuineUp OR freshLicL
//        zoneOKL     = inSigZone      OR freshLicL
//    and EMA-Fib confluence was never a gate at all — it only tinted the
//    background. So a reclaim bar could confirm a long with falling EMAs,
//    outside the zone, with no confluence, entirely by design. That design
//    does not match the documented Play 3 read (S✓ required) and it is not
//    what the engine should do.
//    NEW "Strict" block (all three default ON): licenses can no longer bypass
//    slope or zone; EMA-Fib confluence becomes a hard requirement; price
//    itself must be inside the signal zone. Licenses keep their real job —
//    deciding WHEN an EMA test counts as a trigger — but they no longer excuse
//    a condition. Turn all three off to restore v1.8.1 behavior exactly.
//
// 2) THE SLOPE TEST WAS MEASURING NOISE. Slope was `ema > ema[4]` with no
//    magnitude floor. On a 1W chart a 50-week EMA has alpha ~0.039, so a
//    two-bar bounce can tick it up by hundredths of a percent while the line
//    is visibly falling — and that registered as "rising". NEW "Min Slope %
//    over Lookback" (default 0.0 = unchanged) requires the EMA to have MOVED
//    by at least that much across the lookback before it counts as sloped.
//    On weekly charts 0.5-1.0 is the working range; the Signal row tooltip now
//    prints the live slope percentages so you can calibrate it against what
//    you actually see on the chart.
//
// Dashboard gains C✓/C✗ (confluence gate) and P✓/P✗ (price-in-zone gate).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8.1 — DUPLICATE-EMA COLLAPSE (critical)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Fixes a stack-gate defect that made the signal engine behave incorrectly on
// any chart whose timeframe MATCHES one of the signal EMAs' timeframes — most
// commonly a 1W chart with the default weekly-50 in slot 6, where slot 1 and
// slot 6 are literally the same series.
//
//  • v1.7 on such a chart chained slot 6 after slot 3 and required
//    ma1 > ma2 > ma3 > ma6 with ma6 identical to ma1 — an arithmetic
//    contradiction. stackUp AND stackDn were permanently false, so with
//    "Require EMA Stack Order" on (the default) the engine could not fire a
//    single long or short. Silently dead since v1.4 on matching timeframes.
//  • v1.8 ranked by effective length and skipped tied pairs so they could not
//    deadlock — but a tie still CONSUMED a slot of the depth window, deleting
//    a real constraint. On a weekly chart the default depth of 3 collapsed to
//    a single ma1 > ma2 test, so reclaim-bar entries fired that the full chain
//    would have rejected.
//  • v1.8.1 folds duplicates out BEFORE the depth window is built. Every pair
//    inside the window is now an enforceable constraint. Where lengths tie the
//    lowest slot number supplies the value. The dashboard prints the fold as
//    K✓ 3/4 so the collapse is visible rather than inferred.
//  • Stack Order Depth accepts 0 = ALL ranked members, so the gate can never
//    be looser than v1.7 by omission.
//  • NEW "Reclaim Bar Itself Triggers" (default ON = existing behavior). Turn
//    it OFF to require an actual EMA test while keeping the reclaim window as
//    a license — the answer to "this signal never tested anything".
//  • anchorUp no longer defaults to UPTREND when both fib anchor bars are na
//    (the fallback-range case): it falls through to the swing anchors, then to
//    range position, so the signal zone is not silently long-biased in exactly
//    the situation the fallback exists for.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HTF REGIME NAVIGATOR v1.8 — CORRECTNESS PASS
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// This release closes every defect found in the v1.7 audit. Grouped by what
// they change. EVERY EXISTING INPUT TITLE IS UNCHANGED, so saved settings on
// live charts survive the update; all new controls are additive.
//
// ── SIGNAL-CHANGING FIXES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  1. FRESH-STACK LICENSE (was firing mid-trend). v1.7 granted a 15-bar
//     gate-bypass on ANY transition into stacked order, including a one-bar
//     EMA1/EMA2 wobble inside an established trend — which voided the slope
//     AND zone gates and turned a reversal engine into a continuation engine.
//     The stack license now requires QUALIFICATION: "Fresh-Stack
//     Qualification" (default: the stack must have been broken for >= N bars)
//     with "Full Opposite Stack" (a literal recross, matching the Playbook's
//     "EMAs flip from bearish to bullish order") and "Any Re-stack" (v1.7
//     behavior) as alternatives.
//  2. WYCKOFF LICENSE ASYMMETRY. wyckFresh satisfied freshLic (bypassing the
//     slope and zone gates) but was absent from testElig, so a spring-only
//     license produced a SETUP that could never trigger. Added to both.
//  3. SHORT TEST ENTRIES WERE UNREACHABLE. In asymmetric mode bearState
//     required every signal EMA BELOW, but the tolerance band that DEFINES a
//     test assigns status 0 (TESTING), not -1 — so the qualifying bar was the
//     disqualifying bar. bearState now permits EMA1 inside the test band.
//  4. STACK ORDER IS NOW TIMEFRAME-AWARE. v1.7 compared timeframe strings and
//     dropped every non-chart-TF EMA from the ordering. EMAs are now ranked by
//     EFFECTIVE LENGTH (length x timeframe / chart timeframe) and the fastest
//     N ("Stack Order Depth", default 3) must be in strict order. At defaults
//     this is byte-identical to v1.7 (50 > 100 > 200) but it no longer breaks
//     on TF-string normalisation and it correctly places the weekly-50 as the
//     ~350-day member instead of guessing from slot position.
//  5. SIGNAL ZONE DECOUPLED FROM THE DISCRETIONARY OVERLAY. The signal fib
//     orientation followed netScore, so a Macro Liquidity dropdown could move
//     the mechanical entry geometry. New "Signal Zone Orientation" defaults to
//     Structure Only (regime, else which anchor formed last). Set it to
//     "Follow Display Fib" to restore v1.7.
//  6. WYCKOFF LICENSE WINDOW. The spring license ran on Event Memory (30
//     bars) — 30 bars is not "fresh". Own control, default 10.
//
// ── NEW: EXIT & ATTRIBUTION LAYER ──────────────────────────────────────────
//  7. SIGNAL END. At defaults INVALIDATED almost never fired: it needs a close
//     below the LOWEST signal EMA (often the weekly-50), while the far more
//     common exit — losing EMA1 — took the silent state-0 branch with no
//     alert, no marker, no bus event. Added: sigEnd event + alert + optional
//     marker + bus stream, plus an "Invalidation Reference" selector so the
//     INVALIDATED state can be made meaningful.
//  8. ENTRY ROUTE ATTRIBUTION. EXT: Entry Signal Confirmed emitted an
//     undifferentiated +/-1 across three structurally different setups. The
//     route (1 reclaim / 2 fresh stack / 3 Wyckoff) is latched at the trigger
//     bar, shown in the dashboard, carried into the alert, and published on
//     "EXT: Entry Route" so the Expectancy Lab can bucket per route.
//  9. STOP REFERENCE BUS. Selectable structural stop (lowest signal EMA /
//     EMA1 / spring-UT extreme / 78.6%) published for the Lab, so brackets can
//     be simulated on the stop the Playbook actually prescribes.
//
// ── CORRECTNESS / ROBUSTNESS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// 10. maActive == 0 inverted the consolidation prior (0 >= 0 and 0 >= -1 made
//     every breakout a confirmed trend). Guarded.
// 11. Swing line/label cap kept 2x the stated maximum (`> max * 2`). Fixed —
//     if you preferred the old density, double your "Max Lines" setting.
// 12. Short confluence score was inflated 8 pts (the 22-pt branch re-tested
//     the outer condition and was unreachable in asymmetric mode).
// 13. Slope score awarded a permanent +18 while Slope Alignment was "Off".
// 14. Volume score had no na-guard (volumeless symbols lost 15 pts forever);
//     now scores neutral.
// 15. Volume profile rebuilt on EVERY realtime tick (up to ~150k iterations).
//     Now rebuilds once per bar; "Update Profile Every Tick" restores v1.7.
// 16. Negative bar-index guards on fib line start and swing label offsets.
// 17. VP row-index range guarded against inversion (Pine's for-loop counts
//     DOWN when to < from, and v6 negative array indexing would have written
//     silently to the wrong bucket).
// 18. Zone direction now uses ONE variable end-to-end (biasBull vs
//     biasStickyBull disagreed inside the hysteresis band).
// 19. Zone reference "EMA 1" with EMA1 disabled silently vetoed forever;
//     falls back to price.
// 20. Dashboard: short-side detail showed the LONG metric (nAb+nTe); shows the
//     correct side now. Slope gained S-recycle to distinguish a genuine
//     alignment from a license bypass (mirrors Z-recycle). Stack gained K-dash
//     for "not applicable". Range cell carries an age/validity tooltip.
//
// ── TRADING LOGIC ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 21. RANGE FALLBACK. An inverted fractal pair blanked rangeValid, which
//     killed the regime engine, the golden zone and every non-reclaim signal
//     mode until a new fractal formed. Optional fallback to the HTF
//     highest/lowest over the context lookback (default ON) keeps the engine
//     alive; the dashboard states which source is live.
// 22. SPRING RECLAIM WINDOW. Springs required a same-bar reclaim; classic
//     springs often close below and reclaim on bar 2-3. Configurable window
//     (default 1 = v1.7 behavior).
// 23. SESSION-RELATIVE VOLUME. Optional intraday RVOL baseline (same slot of
//     session, EMA over N sessions) so the Wyckoff "stopping volume" test is
//     not trivially easy at the open and close. Default SMA = v1.7 behavior.
// 24. BELOW-TOLERANCE MULTIPLIER. A close 0.4% under EMA1 (tolerance 0.3%)
//     killed the whole setup — a normal test candle on a volatile name.
//     Optional wider band for the BELOW classification only (default 1.0 =
//     unchanged).
// 25. REGIME ALERT TIMING. HTF values develop intrabar, so REGIME CHANGE could
//     fire mid-week and reverse. Default now compares the regime at successive
//     HTF closes; "Every Bar" restores v1.7.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// REPAINT POLICY: all security() calls use lookahead_off. Fractals confirm
// after <wings> HTF bars (Williams definition) — late by design, never
// repainted. HTF values develop on the live HTF candle and finalise on its
// close; note that this means an HTF-derived state can differ between a live
// session and the same bar re-read from history. Signal markers and all
// alerts are bar-close gated. As of v1.8 the regime alert is HTF-close gated
// by default for the same reason.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUTS — HTF MA Stack (6 slots)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

i_ma1On  = input.bool(true, "", inline="m1", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma1TF  = input.timeframe("", "", inline="m1", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma1Len = input.int(50, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m1", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma1Col = input.color(color.aqua, "", inline="m1", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 1. Each of the 6 slots is independent — its own timeframe (blank = chart), length, color, and on/off. Default: chart 50 (aqua), the fast trend EMA; a core member of the signal and confluence checks.")
i_ma2On  = input.bool(true, "", inline="m2", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma2TF  = input.timeframe("", "", inline="m2", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma2Len = input.int(100, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m2", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma2Col = input.color(#8B0000, "", inline="m2", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 2. Default: chart 100 (deep red), the medium trend EMA; core signal/confluence member.")
i_ma3On  = input.bool(true, "", inline="m3", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma3TF  = input.timeframe("", "", inline="m3", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma3Len = input.int(200, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m3", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma3Col = input.color(#00008B, "", inline="m3", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 3. Default: chart 200 (deep blue), the slow trend filter. Price position is measured against it, but its SLOPE is excluded from the reversal check by default (the slowest EMA turns last).")
i_ma4On  = input.bool(false, "", inline="m4", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma4TF  = input.timeframe("1W", "", inline="m4", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma4Len = input.int(200, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m4", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma4Col = input.color(color.white, "", inline="m4", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 4. Default: weekly 200 (white), DISPLAY OFF — a higher-timeframe context line you can switch on anytime.")
i_ma5On  = input.bool(false, "", inline="m5", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma5TF  = input.timeframe("1D", "", inline="m5", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma5Len = input.int(50, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m5", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma5Col = input.color(color.white, "", inline="m5", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 5. Default: daily 50 (white), DISPLAY OFF — extra context, off by default to keep the chart clean.")
i_ma6On  = input.bool(true, "", inline="m6", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma6TF  = input.timeframe("1W", "", inline="m6", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma6Len = input.int(50, "EMA", minval=2, maxval=500, inline="m6", group="HTF MA Stack")
i_ma6Col = input.color(#FA8072, "", inline="m6", group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="EMA slot 6. Default: weekly 50 (salmon), shown and in the signal set — your higher-timeframe trend anchor. v1.8: the stack ORDER check now ranks every signal EMA by EFFECTIVE length (length x its timeframe / the chart timeframe), so this slot is correctly treated as the ~350-day member on a daily chart. It sits outside the default Stack Order Depth of 3, which is why it no longer blocks fresh reversals — raise the depth if you want it included.")
i_maW        = input.int(2, "Line Width", minval=1, maxval=5, group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="Line thickness for all six EMAs.")
i_maSlopeLen = input.int(4, "Slope Lookback (HTF bars)", minval=1, maxval=20, group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="HTF bars used to measure each MA's slope for trend confirmation")
i_maSlopeMinPct = input.float(0.0, "Min Slope % over Lookback", minval=0.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.05, group="HTF MA Stack", tooltip="v1.8.2. An EMA only counts as RISING (or FALLING) if it has moved by at least this percentage across the Slope Lookback. 0.0 = the old behavior, a bare `ema > ema[n]` comparison. Why it matters: a 50-period EMA has a smoothing factor near 0.04, so on a weekly chart a two-bar bounce can lift it by a few hundredths of a percent while the line is visibly rolling over — and that counted as a positive slope, which is the usual reason a long fires against an obviously falling EMA. Suggested starting points: 0.5-1.0 on weekly, 0.25-0.5 on daily, 0.1-0.25 intraday. The Signal row tooltip prints the live slope percentages so you can set this against real numbers rather than guessing. Applies to both the signal engine and the regime MA confirmation.")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUTS — HTF Range / Williams Fractals
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

i_rangeTF      = input.timeframe("1W", "Range Timeframe", group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="Timeframe whose Williams fractals define the trading range. Weekly per your process.")
i_fracWings    = input.int(2, "Fractal Wings", minval=1, maxval=10, group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="Williams fractal = pivot with N bars on each side. 2 = classic Williams. Confirms N HTF bars after the extreme — late by design, never repaints.")
i_rangeBufPct  = input.float(0.5, "Breakout Buffer %", minval=0.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="HTF close must exceed range high/low by this % to count as a breakout")
i_ctxLookback  = input.int(26, "Range Context Lookback (HTF bars)", minval=4, maxval=200, group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="How far back (in range-TF bars) to check where price came FROM. Fell into range = accumulation candidate, rose into range = distribution candidate. 26 = ~6 months on weekly. v1.8: also defines the fallback range window.")
i_rangeFallback = input.bool(true, "Fallback Range If Fractals Invert", group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="v1.8: when a new fractal LOW confirms ABOVE the stale fractal HIGH (a V-move), the fractal pair is incoherent and v1.7 blanked the whole range — which silently disabled the regime engine, the golden zone, the entry-value component and every non-reclaim signal mode until a new fractal formed. With this ON the range falls back to the highest high / lowest low over the Range Context Lookback on the range timeframe, and the dashboard Range row marks it FB. Turn OFF to keep the strict v1.7 behavior (engine goes quiet instead of approximating).")
i_rangeLinesOn = input.bool(true, "Draw Range High/Low", group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="Draw the horizontal range high/low lines on the chart. The range is still computed for the regime engine even if these lines are hidden.")
i_rangeHiCol   = input.color(color.new(color.red, 0), "High", inline="rc", group="HTF Range (Fractals)")
i_rangeLoCol   = input.color(color.new(color.green, 0), "Low", inline="rc", group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="Colors of the range boundary lines (high and low).")
i_rangeW       = input.int(2, "Width", minval=1, maxval=5, inline="rs", group="HTF Range (Fractals)")
i_rangeStyle   = input.string("Solid", "", options=["Solid", "Dashed", "Dotted"], inline="rs", group="HTF Range (Fractals)", tooltip="Width and line style of the range boundary lines.")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUTS — Swing Detection (ported from MFTA Suite, native HTF pivots)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

i_showSwings    = input.bool(true, "Show Swing Levels", group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Draws horizontal lines at confirmed Williams pivots. Detection runs regardless of this toggle (so the Swing H/L fib anchor keeps working); this only controls the drawing.")
i_swingTF       = input.timeframe("", "Timeframe", group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Leave blank for current chart timeframe. Pivots are computed natively on this timeframe.")
i_swingLookback = input.int(5, "Lookback Bars", minval=1, maxval=50, group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Measured in bars of the SWING timeframe (not chart bars)")
i_maxSwingLines = input.int(10, "Max Lines", minval=1, maxval=50, group="Swing Detection", tooltip="How many recent swing lines to keep on the chart before the oldest are removed. v1.8 BUG FIX: v1.7 pruned at 2x this number, so a setting of 10 actually kept 20 lines. The setting is now literal — if you preferred the old density, double it.")
i_swingHighCol  = input.color(color.red, "High Color", inline="swc", group="Swing Detection")
i_swingLowCol   = input.color(color.green, "Low Color", inline="swc", group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Colors of the swing-high and swing-low lines.")
i_swingWidth    = input.int(1, "Width", minval=1, maxval=5, inline="sws", group="Swing Detection")
i_swingStyle    = input.string("Solid", "", options=["Solid", "Dashed", "Dotted"], inline="sws", group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Width and line style of the swing lines.")
i_swingExtend   = input.bool(true, "Extend Right", group="Swing Detection", tooltip="Extend swing lines to the right edge of the chart instead of stopping at the confirmation bar.")

// Swing Labels
i_showSwingLbl    = input.bool(true, "Show Labels", group="Swing Labels", tooltip="Show a price/level label on each swing line.")
i_swingLblSize    = input.string("Small", "Size", options=["Tiny", "Small", "Normal", "Large"], group="Swing Labels", tooltip="Text size of the swing labels.")
i_swingLblFormat  = input.string("Level & Price", "Format", options=["Price Only", "Level Only", "Level & Price"], group="Swing Labels", tooltip="What each swing label shows: just the price, just the prefix (SH/SL), or both.")
i_swingHighPrefix = input.string("SH", "High Prefix", inline="swp", group="Swing Labels")
i_swingLowPrefix  = input.string("SL", "Low Prefix", inline="swp", group="Swing Labels", tooltip="Text prefix shown before each swing-high (SH) and swing-low (SL) label.")
i_swingLblAlpha   = input.int(80, "Bg Alpha", minval=0, maxval=100, group="Swing Labels", tooltip="Background transparency of the swing labels (0 = solid, 100 = invisible background).")
i_swingLblOffset  = input.int(0, "Offset", minval=-50, maxval=50, group="Swing Labels", tooltip="Shift swing labels left (-) or right (+) from the line, in bars.")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUTS — Wyckoff Engine (rebuilt, fully customizable)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

i_wyckOn        = input.bool(true, "Enable Wyckoff Engine", group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Master switch for spring/upthrust detection and the Wyckoff volume character used by the regime and forecast.")
i_springMaxPen  = input.float(15.0, "Max Penetration % of Range", minval=1.0, maxval=50.0, step=1.0, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="A spring/upthrust must poke through the range edge by NO MORE than this % of range height. Deeper = treated as a genuine break, not a shakeout.")
i_springReclaimBars = input.int(1, "Reclaim Window (bars)", minval=1, maxval=10, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="v1.8: how many bars the reclaim may take. 1 = the undercut and the reclaim close must be the SAME bar (v1.7 behavior). Classic springs often close below the low and reclaim on bar 2-3 — set 2-3 to capture those. The volume qualification is evaluated on the UNDERCUT bar, which is where the Wyckoff information actually is.")
i_wyckVolMode   = input.string("Any", "Volume Qualification", options=["Any", "Low Volume Test (<avg)", "Stopping Volume (>mult x avg)"], group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Classic Wyckoff: a LOW-volume undercut = no supply (quiet test). A HIGH-volume undercut that reclaims = stopping volume / absorption. 'Any' accepts both.")
i_wyckVolSmaLen = input.int(20, "Volume SMA Length", minval=5, maxval=100, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Bars used to compute the average volume that the spring/upthrust volume test compares against. Ignored when the shared Volume Baseline is set to Session Relative.")
i_wyckVolMult   = input.float(1.5, "Stopping Volume Multiplier", minval=1.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="For 'Stopping Volume' mode: the undercut bar's volume must exceed this multiple of average volume to qualify.")
i_wyckCooldown  = input.int(5, "Signal Cooldown (bars)", minval=0, maxval=100, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Minimum bars between consecutive spring (or upthrust) signals — stops repeat-firing when price oscillates around the range edge")
i_volLookback   = input.int(60, "Effort/Result Volume Lookback", minval=10, maxval=300, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Chart bars used to compare up-bar vs down-bar volume inside the range")
i_volBias       = input.float(0.55, "Volume Bias Threshold", minval=0.5, maxval=0.8, step=0.01, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Up-volume share above this = accumulation character; below (1 - this) = distribution character")
i_eventMemory   = input.int(30, "Event Memory (chart bars)", minval=5, maxval=200, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="How long a Spring/Upthrust/Divergence keeps influencing the regime & forecast. v1.8: this NO LONGER governs the signal engine's Wyckoff license — that has its own, shorter window in the Signal Engine group.")
i_wyckEventPts  = input.int(50, "Forecast Pts: Spring/Upthrust", minval=0, maxval=100, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="How many points a recent spring (+) or upthrust (-) contributes to the Wyckoff component of the forecast. NOTE: one spring also moves the Regime component and the Entry Value multiplier — the six forecast components are not statistically independent.")
i_wyckVolPts    = input.int(100, "Forecast Pts: Volume Share (max)", minval=0, maxval=100, group="Wyckoff Engine", tooltip="Max contribution of the up/down volume share to the Wyckoff component score")

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// INPUTS — Volume Baseline (shared by Wyckoff qualification + signal gate)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

i_volBaseMode = input.string("SMA", "Volume Baseline", options=["SMA", "Session Relative"], group="Volume Baseline (Shared)", tooltip="SMA (default, v1.7 behavior) = flat moving average of volume. SESSION RELATIVE = compare each bar to the average volume of the SAME slot of the session over the last N sessions. On intraday charts a flat SMA is distorted by the U-shaped session curve, which makes 'Stopping Volume' trivially easy at the open and close and nearly impossible at midday. Session Relative removes that bias. Ignored on non-intraday charts (falls back to SMA automatically).")
i_volTodDays  = input.int(20, "Session Relative: Sessions", minval=2, maxval=200, group="Volume Baseline (Shared)", tooltip="How many sessions of memory the session-relative baseline keeps (exponential). 20 = roughly a month of intraday sessions.")

// Wyckoff markers (labels — compact & styleable)
i_wyckMarkOn   = input.bool(true, "Show Markers", group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="Show the spring/upthrust labels on the chart (detection still runs if hidden).")
i_springTxt    = input.string("SPRING", "Spring Text", inline="wt", group="Wyckoff Markers")
i_utTxt        = input.string("UT", "Upthrust Text", inline="wt", group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="Label text drawn at a detected spring and upthrust.")
i_springCol    = input.color(color.lime, "Spring", inline="wc", group="Wyckoff Markers")
i_utCol        = input.color(color.red, "Upthrust", inline="wc", group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="Colors of the spring and upthrust labels.")
i_wyckLblSize  = input.string("Tiny", "Size", options=["Tiny", "Small", "Normal", "Large"], inline="ws", group="Wyckoff Markers")
i_wyckLblAlpha = input.int(70, "Bg Alpha", minval=0, maxval=100, inline="ws", group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="Label size and background transparency for the Wyckoff markers.")
i_maxWyckLbls  = input.int(20, "Max Markers", minval=2, maxval=100, group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="How many recent Wyckoff markers to keep before the oldest are removed.")
i_wyckMarkOffset = input.float(0.0, "Marker Offset (ATR)", minval=0.0, maxval=10.0, step=0.1, group="Wyckoff Markers", tooltip="v1.8.9. Pushes SPRING/UT labels away from the candle by this many ATRs (spring below the low, upthrust above the high). 0 = touching the bar, the previous behavior. 0.5-1.0 is usually enough to stop labels overlapping wicks; ATR-denominated so the same setting works across symbols and timeframes.")

